{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q005_cupredoxin_function_transfer","answer":"SCOPe 2.08 places all three groups in the same superfamily: Cupredoxins, b.6.1 (sunid 49503), within the Cupredoxin-like fold b.6 (sunid 49502; beta-sandwich, 7 strands in 2 sheets, Greek key) in class b (all-beta proteins). They are separate families: Plastocyanin/azurin-like is b.6.1.1 (sunid 49504, annotated as mono-domain proteins); Ephrin ectodomain is b.6.1.5 (sunid 74874, Pfam PF00812); and Peptidylarginine deiminase Pad4, N-terminal domain is b.6.1.6 (sunid 110107, Pfam PF08526). The superfamily also contains cytochrome c oxidase subunit II periplasmic domain (b.6.1.2), multidomain cupredoxins (b.6.1.3), nitrosocyanin (b.6.1.4), SO1698-like (b.6.1.7) and automated matches (b.6.1.0). The fold b.6 has a second superfamily, SAG1 major surface antigen p30 (b.6.2), which shares only the fold and is not claimed to be homologous.

Evolutionary strength: family-level grouping implies clear sequence/structure/function evidence of common ancestry; superfamily-level grouping is SCOPe's judgement of probable common ancestry based on structural and functional features despite low sequence identity; fold-level grouping implies only shared topology, not homology. Because plastocyanin/azurin, ephrins and the PAD4 N-terminal domain are in different families but one superfamily, SCOPe asserts a probable but distant homology, not close relationship. SCOPe's own comments hedge this: the ephrin family is described as a 'eukaryotic signaling domain probably related to cupredoxins but lacking the metal-binding site', and the PAD4 N-terminal family as 'probably related to cupredoxins but lacking the metal-binding site'.

Function transfer: copper binding and electron transfer should NOT be inferred across these families. The superfamily comment 'contains copper-binding site' describes the canonical cupredoxins (plastocyanin, azurin, pseudoazurin, amicyanin, CuA domains of cytochrome c oxidase subunit II, multicopper oxidase domains), not every member. Ephrin ectodomains are cell-surface Eph-receptor ligands and the PAD4 N-terminal domain is a non-catalytic domain of a calcium-dependent arginine deiminase; both are explicitly annotated as lacking the His/Cys/His/Met type-1 copper ligand set, so there is no basis for predicting metal binding or redox activity in them from the classification alone. Superfamily membership means shared ancestry and shared beta-sandwich architecture, not shared ligands or chemistry; functions diverge within superfamilies, and even within the cupredoxin superfamily metal sites vary (e.g., nitrosocyanin is a red copper protein). Conversely, the ephrin and PAD4 annotations should not be back-transferred to cast doubt on copper binding in b.6.1.1. To claim copper binding or electron transfer in any specific uncharacterized member one needs independent evidence: conservation of the four ligand residues in a sequence alignment to a characterized cupredoxin, an experimental structure or validated model showing an intact metal site, and biochemical data (metal content, EPR/UV-vis spectroscopy, redox potential). Templates from b.6.1.1 may be used for modeling the ephrin or PAD4 sandwich core, but loop regions and the former metal site region should not be assumed conserved, and residue-level functional annotation from the template should not be carried over.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/b.6","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/b.6/children?limit=30","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/b.6.1/children?limit=30","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/49503","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/49503/annotations","/api/v1/